Step 2's first three clauses are in and pass the full check: tests passed (7727, 0 failed), clippy found no warnings, and the format check is clean. All of it is in commit 4c85a2ae, titled "Part of #1202"; the session before mine wrote it and was cut off before its final check, which I ran here.

What changed for users: an allow rule now auto-approves a compound command only if it covers each command in it on its own. Anything else asks: redirections, substitutions, subshells, loops, or a command no rule covers.

• Now asks (auto before): cargo test 2>&1 under cargo * 2>&1, and git status && ls under git * && ls. More generally, an allow pattern that spelled out a redirect, heredoc, substitution, subshell or chain no longer approves that shape.
• Now auto (asked before): chains, pipelines and newline-separated runs of plain commands that are each covered, such as cargo build && cargo test under cargo *. The task file said that command "must stay auto", but it actually asked before this change, so this is a new approval.
• Unchanged: session "always" approvals, --yes/-p runs, deny rules (still win), and step 1's "ask on an unreadable command". A command the safety check warns about is approved only if both the old whole-text rule and the new per-command rule approve it, so nothing it warns about becomes newly auto-approved.

Record:
• The commit body has the clause table, each newly asking or newly auto-approved command, and one limit: a command written with quotes, like git "status", asks under an exact git status rule.
• The before-table and the after-table with the recall control were posted on #1202 before the full check (comments 7194056601 and 7194270169). Each comment names the deferral in its first 200 characters.
• Recall control: forcing the new check to always approve turned 3 tests red by name; once restored, all 31 passed. No NEUTERED marker is left in the tree.
• Docs: docs/src/configuration/permissions.md now says an allow rule must cover every command, lists the changes, and drops the two sentences that told users to spell the chain or redirect into the pattern.

